narrative:
## Combining Interfaces

You've used individual interfaces. Now combine them to demand multiple behaviors at once.

### Two Simple Interfaces

```xiom
interface Readable {
  fn read(&self) -> Str;
}

interface Writable {
  fn write(&mut self, data: Str);
}
```

### A Type That Does Both

```xiom
type Document {
  content: Str;
}

fn Document.read(&self) -> Str {
  self.content.clone()
}

fn Document.write(&mut self, data: Str) {
  self.content = data;
}
```

### Requiring Both with +

```xiom
fn process[T: Readable + Writable](doc: &mut T) {
  let text = doc.read();
  doc.write(text);
}
```

`T: Readable + Writable` means: T must implement BOTH interfaces. The `+` acts like AND.

### When to Use Interface Composition

- Keep interfaces small and focused (Interface Segregation Principle)
- Combine them with `+` when a function needs multiple capabilities
- A type can implement many interfaces independently

